176: <a id="decided-2026-11-05"></a>
177: ## Owner decisions 2026-11-05: Core design (A2a provisional approval) and project rows
178: 
179: The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
180: 
181: **The Core rows are a provisional approval (stage A2a).** They bind the design without freezing it. Stage A2b formalizes them in a proposed `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` and proves the four design theorems; any row the formalization changes returns to the owner; stage A2i integrates the parts (2026-11-06); the owner freezes Core at A2c (row Q9.19 as revised). Until a rule is written in Lean, the answer below governs over any chapter text that differs.
182: 
183: <a id="decided-2026-11-05-revisions"></a>**Owner-approved revisions of recorded rules** (each also noted at the rule it revises):
184: 
185: - **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
186: - **TYPE-22@1's examples** (row Q6.4): a constant whose initializer provably fails is refused at compile time whether or not it is used (OD118); both examples, `unused-constant-never-runs` and `constant-traps-at-use`, become reject examples. When a constant is evaluated (at each use, never at start) is unchanged.
187: - **TRAIT-1@1** (row Q2.8): two impls with equal heads may coexist only when Copy/Destroy disjointness (OD28) makes their domains disjoint, as in `impl<T: Copy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>` beside `impl<T: Destroy> Destroy for Vec<T, A>`. No other coherence or orphan rule changes, and no disjunctive bound syntax is added.
188: - **The generated-chapter requirement** (rows Q9.15, P3): Lean is the specification, in one main normative file with doc comments and examples; no prose is generated from it. This confirms the [2026-11-04 decision](#decided-project-lean-is-the-spec). The "one main normative file" wording is revised 2026-11-06 by the [long-term changes](#decided-project-long-term-2026-11-06): one authoritative specification library in cohesive modules, with `ConcreteSpec/Spec.lean` as its public import.
189: - **Authority rules 13, 5 and 7** (row Q7.11): `Console` joins the reserved capability set, for operations on the `StdIn`, `StdOut` and `StdErr` grants; rule 5's operational list and rule 7's primitive table include it. `Device` stays for device grants.
190: - **The text-only slice of R4 F4.3** (row Q4.7) is replaced before it was recorded: one scoped form opens every view, `borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]`. Without `else` an invalid range traps, like indexing; with `else` it is handled; a proven invalid range without `else` is refused (OD118). Arrays need `a ≤ b ≤ len`; text also needs UTF-8 scalar boundaries at both byte offsets.
191: - <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields"></a>**Q6.2 and R4 F12, known values through structs** (owner-approved revision, 2026-11-05, made during A2b): known values propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring. The checker tracks an abstract value for each binding: unknown, a known scalar, or a constructor whose fields carry their own abstract values. Constructing a struct records each field's fact independently; an immutable binding keeps the fact; destructuring hands each field's fact, recursively, to the corresponding immutable binder; an unknown component stays unknown without discarding its known siblings; an ownership transfer keeps the facts for the receiving binding without permitting reuse of the consumed one. Q6.2's exclusions stay (mutable state, branches and paths, user-function calls, calls outside the table, elaborator temporaries). So `let S { a } = S { a: 200u8 }; a + 100` is refused, in trusted and unreachable code too (OD118 as revised by Q6.4). Before this revision a field bound by destructuring was not known and that program was accepted and trapped.
192: - <a id="decided-2026-11-05-q9-1-reads"></a>**Q9.1, read forms** (owner-confirmed, 2026-11-05): `copy x` is allowed only when `x` has a Copy type and `move x` only when it has a non-Copy (linear) type. Each read has one form; `move` of a Copy value and `copy` of a linear value are both refused.
193: 

---
221: - **Q3.3** What is a view’s root identity? — Original owner identity plus projection path.
222: - **Q3.4** Which view accesses conflict? — Existing whole-root conflict rules.
223: - **Q3.5** What is the index type? — `u64` lengths/indices across profiles; checked target limits.
224: - **Q3.6** How are nested linear elements traversed without substitutes? — `drain` / `map_into` for consuming traversal; `swap_at(v, i, x) -> T` for in-place replacement.
225: - **Q3.7** What is the raw-memory model and validity taxonomy? — Blocks with provenance; pointer = (prov: Option<BlockId>, addr: u64); integers carry no provenance; every access needs explicit validity premises. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the address is read from a read-only target profile; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile is unchanged, and the provenance rules and the zero-byte exception are preserved.*
226: - **Q3.8** How do zero-sized elements behave? — Zero-sized slots own their obligations; they occupy no bytes and may share addresses.
227: - **Q3.9** What do pointer equality and integer round trips mean? — `expose` records the block; `from_exposed` picks in-bounds, then one-past, else no provenance. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): over the target profile's address spaces; the reconstruction policy is unchanged.*
228: - **Q3.10** What happens when address space runs out? — A collision means overlapping occupied bytes; address exhaustion is part of Avail. *Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): the existing profile keeps the `base + size ≤ 2^64−1` one-past bound; any generalized profile needs an analogous checked bound.*
229: - **Q3.11** How are typed values related to raw bytes? — Structured values and raw store joined by `Rep`.
230: - **Q3.12** How are extern pointer parameters marked, and how does C keep memory past the call? — (a) `kept` parameter marker; `Kept<H>` receipt ended only by the paired extern. *Revises or adds: New design + spelling: owner decision.*
231: - **Q4.1** What is a literal’s type? — Copy `StaticStr`; explicit fallible dynamic conversion.
232: - **Q4.2** Which encoding and NUL policy apply? — UTF-8 including embedded NUL.
233: - **Q4.3** What do length and comparison mean? — Byte length; content equality; lexicographic byte order.
234: - **Q4.4** Are literal addresses observable and literals mergeable? — Content semantics; pooling allowed; no safe address identity.
235: - **Q4.5** What is the FFI representation? — Explicit pointer/byte-length FFI adapter.
236: - **Q4.6** What are dynamic string views? — Byte offsets; `byte_at` traps out of range; subviews only through the scoped borrow form of Q4.7 (`borrow t from a until b as s { … } [else { … }]`). *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): byte_at needs evidence of an in-range offset.*
237: - **Q4.7** How are scoped views and range subviews introduced? — (a) borrow [mut] source [from a until b] as view { … } [else { … }]; no else = trap (like indexing); proven invalid range without else refused (OD118). *Revises or adds: Replaces R4 F4.3 text-only mandatory-else form; extends Q3.1/Q3.2 with subview formation; preserves OD36/OD118.* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): the form without `else` needs evidence of a valid range; a provably out-of-range view index is covered by the same rule.*
238: - **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
239: - **Q5.2** May explicit arguments contain the caller’s capability variables? — Explicit symbolic capability expressions and union.
240: - **Q5.3** What is capability-set equality and normal form? — Canonical semantic equality; refuse repeated names.
241: - **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
242: - **Q6.1** What determines mandatory static refusal? — Fixed Lean-defined local refusal rules. *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): static refusal widens from proven violations to unproven safety.*
243: - **Q6.2** Is local constant propagation mandatory? — Fixed table of statically known values: literals, immutable lets, module constants, checked ops, six named wrapping/saturating ops, lossless `as`. *Revised 2026-11-05: facts propagate field by field through immutable struct construction and destructuring ([revision](#decided-2026-11-05-q6-2-fields)).* *Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): known values remain facts; refusal widens to unproven safety.*
244: - **Q6.3** Are violations rechecked after generic substitution? — Parametric checking plus substitution-time rechecking.
245: - **Q6.4** Does unreachable code receive static refusal checks? — Check all written code, including generic bodies. *Revises or adds: REVISES TYPE-22@1 examples.*
246: - **Q6.5** How do short-circuit operators interact with refusal? — Check both short-circuit operands; execute conditionally.

