1289: | decision | proposal | record today | gates | owner's answer |
1290: |---|---|---|---|---|
1291: | OD1 | <a id="od1"></a>References: the Austral model. A reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; loans are keyed by the root variable; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise | D2 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-5, REF-11) | exit 2i (G12), so every deliverable; bug 182's repair (exit 0b); V2 (row 6) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the Austral model: a reference is formed by borrowing a variable or projecting through a reference; `&s.f` is sugar; a temporary is never a root; references are second-class, never stored or returned; a raw-pointer dereference needs a trusted validity premise; loans are keyed by the root variable (confirmed by OD15 (a), decided 2026-10-29) |
1292: | OD2 | <a id="od2"></a>`&mut` arguments: an implicit reborrow; overlapping exclusive arguments refused; a deferred call keeps its loan until it runs; one defined order for cleanup, loan end and write-back | D1 and §4.5; the reborrow implemented in landing train 6 (REF-10); the defer and exit-order rules not yet | exit 2i (G9, B1, B2), so every deliverable; R-0496 (Core reborrow form) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) passing a `&mut` on is plain lending, each call's use ending when it returns, with no reborrow concept or syntax; (2) a call is refused when an exclusive argument overlaps any other argument (`h(r, r)`, `h(&mut x, &x)`, `h(&mut a, &mut a.f)` refused); as tightened by OD15 (a) the same day, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, so even `h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused; (3) `&mut` refers to the place, a write through it is the write to that place at once, and copy-in/copy-out is not part of the language (only an optimization proven equivalent); (4) `defer` keeps OWN-12 and a borrow passed to a deferred call stays live until the call runs; (5) one exit sequence on every non-abort exit: the exit value, then the scope's deferred calls (last registered first, innermost scope first), then the scope's loans end, then the remaining locals are destroyed; an abort runs none of it **Canonical current root-based exclusivity rule: REF-11 and OD15(a); this historical overlap formulation is a reference to that rule.** |
1293: | OD3 | <a id="od3"></a>Pattern binders through a borrowed scrutinee: the binder is always `&T`; its mode is stored in Core; the root cannot be reassigned while a binder's loan lives (the rule that reopened G17's Copy case) | D3 and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (REF-9), and with it the refusal of G17's Copy case (closed 2026-10-23 in the references chapter of the specification) | exits 2i (G17) and 2k (explicit match binders), so every deliverable; bug 181's repair (exit 0b); R-0496 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the binder is always `&T`; its mode is stored in Core; the root cannot be reassigned while a binder's loan lives. Already enforced (REF-9, PASS-2, REF-8) |
1294: | OD4 | <a id="od4"></a>Foreign function values: all refused now; typed `extern "C" fn` types later | D4; the refusal implemented in landing train 6 (FFI-1); the typed form not built | bug 166's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0285's callback companion example; R-0283, R-0287 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: all foreign function values are refused now; typed `extern "C" fn` types may come later |
1295: | OD5 | <a id="od5"></a>Generics: a bare `<T>` means Any; `T: Copy` is explicit; Copy for a generic struct is declared and checked, never derived | D5 (today's behaviour, made normative) and D6 with its refinement (a), under which a generic declared `Copy` is Copy when every argument is; D6 implemented 2026-10-23 (OWN-D1, TYPE-14) | R-0499 and R-0510 (the Core checker and checked specialization, row 4); V5 (row 6) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a bare `<T>` means Any and `T: Copy` is explicit; Copy for a generic struct or enum is declared and checked, never derived, and a generic declared Copy is Copy when every argument is |
1296: | OD6 | <a id="od6"></a>Traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity | D7; implemented by bug 186's repair (TRAIT-1 – TRAIT-9) | bug 186's repair (exit 0b), so every deliverable; R-0548, R-0549; V5 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: traits are coherent: one impl per (trait, type), the orphan rule, an ambiguous method call must be qualified, resolution by typed identity. Completed 2026-11-02: qualified as `Trait::method(&x, ...)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current qualification rule: this OD6 as completed by the audit below; the audit supplies syntax, not a second rule.** |
1297: | OD7 | <a id="od7"></a>An unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`), which never confers trust by itself; a gate lists the marked modules | D10; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509's trusted-base inventory; RocksDB slice 1 (where its `extern` declarations live); V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: an unsafe-module marker in source (for example `trusted mod`) never confers trust by itself, a gate lists the marked modules, and trust stays per method (OD22) **Revised 2026-11-03: this historical module-marker requirement was superseded by OD61 (2026-11-01), which removed `trusted mod`; trust and inventory remain per declaration.** |
1298: | OD8 | <a id="od8"></a>Global state: immutable globals everywhere; mutable state only as declared cells in unsafe-marked modules, reached through capabilities; no `static mut` | D8; pending (TRUST-6, R-0509) | R-0509, R-0496; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: Austral's capability model, extended. (1) Constants only: no mutable global state of any kind (no `static mut`, no cells); a constant is truly immutable, has no interior mutability and never contains a capability, token or handle. (2) Global resources are linear tokens minted at startup: `main` receives a root resource value (in the spirit of Austral's `RootCapability`) and splits it; hardware and runtime singletons (allocator arena, UART and MMIO, panic buffer, errno, C libraries with internal state) are produced or taken only by `trusted fn` wrappers returning or taking linear tokens, and a gate lists those wrappers. (3) Tokens are ordinary values: every effect shows as a token parameter, under the existing loan and exclusivity rules (OD1, OD2, OD15), with no separate effect system. (4) Interrupt handlers, FFI callbacks and future threads get only the tokens explicitly given to them, by installation, transfer or a lock granting a borrow. (5) No life before `main`, no cleanup protocol for global state, and abort runs nothing (OD10). (6) An implicit-context mechanism, if ergonomics later demands one, is a new owner decision, never a hidden global. The cell variant was rejected after the Codex and Grok reviews (interrupts, FFI re-entrancy, linear cell contents, effect polymorphism, concurrency). Now TRUST-8 to TRUST-10 (pending, R-0557) Revised 2026-11-01 – 11-02: items 2 and 3 are superseded by the authority decision (grants handed to `main`; `with(...)` plus resource values); item 5 stands ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
1299: | OD9 | <a id="od9"></a>Modules and interfaces: one file per module, `pub` as the interface, interface artifacts generated by the compiler, no handwritten interface files | D9; pending (TIGHTENING_DECISIONS §5, step 6) | R-0492 and R-0497 (interface data); separate compilation in V6; OD20 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: one file per module with `pub` as the interface; interface artifacts generated by the compiler, no handwritten interface files; a gate diffs each module's generated interface between commits so any public-API change shows in review (R-0556) |
1300: | OD10 | <a id="od10"></a>Arithmetic: checked by default, named wrapping and saturating operations, shifts with a checked count; an abort runs no deferred call | T-ARITH, recorded as settled (today's behaviour), with §4.5's abort rule | exits 2h and 2j, so V1a, V1b and V1c (checked `+ - *` and their traps); R-0493 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: integer arithmetic is checked by default; wrapping and saturating arithmetic are named operations; the shift count is checked; an abort runs no deferred call **Revised 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): plain arithmetic is admitted only with evidence that it cannot fail; wrapping, saturating and checked (`Result`) forms cover the rest; overflow is still never silent.** |
1301: | OD11 | <a id="od11"></a>Casts: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function | T-CAST: ARITHMETIC_POLICY §8 (2026-10-21) and §4.5's value-domain rule; not implemented | exit 2k; the V1 increment that admits casts | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: `as` only when lossless by value domain; every lossy conversion is a named function |
1302: | OD12 | <a id="od12"></a>Contracts (H27): a pure, non-consuming specification judgment, with machine and mathematical arithmetic kept distinct | T-H27 and §4.5; pending (R-0473) | R-0473 (row 12); any V1a or V1b claim stated with a contract | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a contract is a pure, non-consuming specification judgment, with machine and mathematical arithmetic kept distinct |
1303: | OD13 | <a id="od13"></a>Raw-pointer ownership: `ptr::read_owned`, an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, a trusted ownership obligation for a raw address | T-RAWDUP and §4.5; implemented in landing train 6 (TRUST-7). **Provisional (bug 198):** the current compiler refuses a by-value `*p` of a non-Copy pointee through a raw pointer (E0311) and moves the value out only with the intrinsic `read_owned(p)`, with 26 std sites migrated; provisional until the owner answers | RocksDB slice 1 (its trusted binding code); R-0509, R-0514; V6 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a value moves out of raw memory only through `read_owned(p)` or an explicit replace or take through `&mut T`, and a raw address carries a trusted ownership obligation; bug 198's provisional repair is final |
1304: | OD14 | <a id="od14"></a>Type validity (G2, G13, G15, G16): one structural well-formedness judgment before specialization; function types that return a reference refused everywhere | T-G2, T-G13, T-G15, T-G16; implemented 2026-10-23 (G13, G15 and G16 recorded closed in the references chapter of the specification) | exit 2i, so every deliverable | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: one structural well-formedness judgment runs before specialization, and function types that return a reference are refused everywhere |
1305: | OD15 | <a id="od15"></a>G10: whole-owner freezing under a shared borrow stays. G19: no pass assumes `&mut` exclusivity, and the operational loan rules are defined now | T-G10 (keep the freeze) and T-G19 (the policy sentence) | exit 2i (the G10 decision record), so every deliverable; R-0496, R-0505 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner. (a) Whole-owner freezing: a live borrow of any part of a value freezes the whole owner, and loans are keyed by the root binding (REF-11 as in the current compiler); this tightens OD2's part 2: at a call, two arguments that borrow the same root conflict whenever one is exclusive, even disjoint fields (`h(&mut a.f, &mut a.g)` is refused, as in the current compiler); OD1's "keyed by the root variable" stands. (b) The strict option: the language guarantees `&mut` exclusivity, which the checker enforces (OD2, OD15a), and no compiler pass or optimization may assume or exploit it until the checker's guarantee is proven in Lean (R-0499 with the Core semantics R-0496); until then the operational loan rules are defined now, G19 included: a pointer or alias derived from a loan is valid only while that loan lives. Now REF-11@3 (partial, bug 319, which OD23 adds) and REF-14 (pending, R-0496) **Canonical current root-based exclusivity subject: REF-11 (including OD23’s re-lent case); OD2 and OD23 refer here rather than defining separate variants.** |
1306: | OD16 | <a id="od16"></a>A struct literal in an `if`, `while` or `match` head is refused unless parenthesized | not in the record. **Provisional (bug 212):** the current compiler reads an uppercase name followed by `{` in an `if`, `while` or `match` head as the start of a struct literal, refuses `if n > LIMIT { … }` with an E0001 that suggests parentheses, and states that case rule in `grammar/concrete.ebnf` and SYN-1; provisional until the owner answers | V1c (V1's surface grammar has conditionals and loops); R-0500 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: refused unless parenthesized, with no exceptions; the uniform rule replaces the uppercase-name heuristic (bug 212's provisional behaviour). Now SYN-4 (pending, bug 318); whether a literal inside a call's argument list in a head counts as parenthesized is OD34 |
1307: | OD17 | <a id="od17"></a>A field write requires a `mut` binding. Bug 216: `s.y = 1` is accepted on a binding not declared `mut`, while `&mut s.y` and `a[i] = v` are refused | not in the record; bug 216 open | bug 216's repair (class 5, R-0499); V2 (aggregates) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: a field write requires a `mut` binding. Now OWN-10@2 (partial; bug 216 implements it) |
1308: | OD18 | <a id="od18"></a>Match arms: the first matching arm in source order wins; an unreachable arm is refused. Today an unguarded catch-all arm is moved to the end (bug 213) | not in the record. **Provisional (bug 213):** the current compiler first moves unguarded catch-all arms after the other arms (CoreCanonicalize), so a specific arm written after a catch-all moves before it and stays selectable; in that Core order, it accepts an arm after the first unguarded catch-all and does not lower it, and does not refuse it; provisional until the owner answers | exit 2k (the match rules) and exit 2c's differential legs; V2 (match) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the first matching arm in source order wins and an unreachable arm is refused; this changes the current compiler, where CoreCanonicalize moves unguarded catch-all arms to the end (bug 213's provisional behaviour) |
1309: | OD19 | <a id="od19"></a>Outside the language list: the scope of [PROVABLE_V1](../legacy/docs/verification/PROVABLE_V1.md), that is, whether discarded statements, guards and ranges are admitted | open; it holds the model-coverage branch (`wip/model-coverage-3`) | that branch's landing; exit 2c's model-diff coverage | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: the provable subset admits discarded statements, guards and ranges, each entering as its Core semantics lands (this unblocks `wip/model-coverage-3`) **Revised 2026-11-03: the guards and ranges fragments were superseded by OD55 and OD56 (2026-10-31), which removed those forms; discarded statements remain subject to the decided discard rules.** |
1310: | OD20 | <a id="od20"></a>Outside the language list: the visibility of std's internal `std.libc` bindings | bug 226 open (a std API decision) | bug 226's repair (MOD-2); OD9 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: std's internal `std.libc` bindings are private to std; programs cannot see them |
1311: | OD21 | <a id="od21"></a>Representation rights of a parent over its child module (added 2026-10-25, from bug 262): when a parent module imports its child's newtype or struct, every stage lets the parent construct the newtype and read and write the struct's private fields, because Check counts the children's declarations among the module's own for construction and field rights; Resolve also lets a parent name a child's newtype as a symbol without an import. Two options: enforce TYPE-11 as written (a newtype or private field is constructed or accessed only inside the module that declares it, so a child is another module), or amend TYPE-11 to grant a parent these rights over the declarations it imports from its children | the behaviour dates from construction rights slice 1 (2026-07-18); it contradicts TYPE-11@1's wording ("outside the module that declares it"); bug 262's record names `tests/programs/module_qualified_impl` as a program that applying TYPE-11 strictly would refuse; the wider impact is not yet measured (no `scripts/tools/rule_impact.sh` scan is recorded); bug 262's record (branch `wip/newtype-ctor-stage`) leaves the question to R-0011 and makes no recommendation | R-0011; the construction-rights fixtures (`module_qualified_impl` and bug 262's `error_262_*` and `regress_262_*` fixtures); bug 262's follow-up (whether Resolve binds a child's newtype in its parent) | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: only the declaring module; a newtype is constructed, and a private field read or written, only inside the module that declares it, and a parent module gets no rights over its child (TYPE-11 enforced as written) |
1312: | OD22 | <a id="od22"></a>Trust on an `impl` block (added 2026-10-25, from bug 189). Bug 189's repair made the parser refuse `trusted impl` (E0001) and moved trust onto each method (`trusted fn`, 175 methods marked), while TRUST-5@1 in [the specification](../legacy/docs/spec/trusted.md#TRUST-5) still says a `trusted fn` or `trusted impl` may perform raw-pointer operations. Two options: keep the refusal and amend TRUST-5 to drop `trusted impl`, or restore `trusted impl` as TRUST-5@1 states it and revert bug 189's refusal | **Provisional (bug 189):** the current compiler refuses `trusted impl` and trusts only methods declared `trusted fn`; the specification text is unchanged, so the current compiler and TRUST-5@1 disagree; bug 189's record cites the 2026-10-23 rule that a marker never confers trust (D10, itself OD7, proposed); provisional until the owner answers | bug 189's repair (exit 0b) and its pins (`error_189_trusted_impl_refused.con`, `check_trusted_method.sh`); TRUST-5; R-0509's trusted-base inventory; OD7 | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: trust is per method, `trusted fn` only; `trusted impl` stays refused, so bug 189's provisional repair is final |

---
1323: | OD33 | <a id="od33"></a>Inline submodules under OD9 (added 2026-10-29, from recording OD9): OD9's answer is one file per module, and today the language also accepts an inline submodule (`mod m { ... }` inside its parent's file, used by most specification examples and many fixtures). Two options: inline submodules stay (a module is one file or one inline block), or every module is its own file and the inline form is refused. Recommendation: every module its own file, as OD9's answer reads; the specification's examples and the fixtures move their inline modules into project examples when exit 2q migrates the corpus. Austral has one module per file pair and no inline modules Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): no inline `mod m { }`: one file per module | the current compiler accepts inline submodules; MOD-7 (pending, R-0556) records OD9 without settling this; OPEN-33 | R-0556; the specification's examples and the fixture corpus | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): No inline `mod`; one file per module |
1324: | OD34 | <a id="od34"></a>A struct literal inside a call's argument list, an index or an array literal within an `if`, `while` or `match` head (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD16): whether it counts as parenthesized under SYN-4. Two readings: only a grouped expression `( expr )` parenthesizes, so `if same(p, P { v: 1 }) { ... }` is refused; or any enclosing bracket does, so it is accepted (Rust's reading). Recommendation: any enclosing bracket, since inside a call's parentheses the head's `{` cannot be meant, and refusing it would require parentheses inside parentheses Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only an enclosing `(` or `[` parenthesizes, stated as one invariant: in a condition head, a `{` at bracket depth zero opens the block | not in the record; the current compiler accepts it (the uppercase heuristic reads a struct literal anywhere in a head) | bug 318's repair (SYN-4); V1c | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only enclosing `(`/`[` parenthesize; `{` at depth 0 opens the block |
1325: | OD35 | <a id="od35"></a>How an array satisfies a `Destroy` bound (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD28, which allows either form): one impl written in std for `[T; N]` with `T: Destroy`, or one language impl that is its own specification entry. Recommendation: the language impl, since an array's destruction is its elements' in order and std cannot write an impl generic over the length without const generics Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): a language impl `[T; N]: Destroy` when `T: Destroy`, elements destroyed in index order, with its own identity in coherence | the current compiler counts an array of destroyable elements as `Destroy` with no impl (bug 324) | bug 324's repair (TRAIT-10) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language impl `[T; N]: Destroy` when `T: Destroy`, index order |
1326: | OD36 | <a id="od36"></a>The spelling of the explicit non-consuming place form that opens a `Heap` or `HeapArray` (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD30, which requires one and keeps `*` as the consuming move-out). Options: a borrow through the cell (`(&h).f`, `(&mut h).f`), a named projection (`h.open().f`), or a dedicated operator. Recommendation: the borrow form, which needs no new syntax and matches OD32's explicit receiver borrow Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): only through a borrow block (`borrow h as r { r.f }`), no `(&h).f`; specify the result type for `Heap<T>`, `&Heap<T>` and `&mut Heap<T>`, one heap layer per block (MEM-4), and `HeapArray` elements | the current compiler opens one heap layer implicitly (`h.f` on `Heap<S>`, bug 326) | bug 326's repair (MEM-4) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Only through a borrow block; no `(&h).f` |
1327: | OD37 | <a id="od37"></a>The names of the pointer difference and ordering functions (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26). Options: `ptr::diff(p, q) -> Int` and `ptr::cmp(p, q) -> Ordering` beside `ptr::offset`; or no ordering at all, only `ptr::addr(p)` (via `ptr::expose`) compared as integers. Recommendation: `ptr::diff` and `ptr::cmp`, both trusted, each with its provenance premise (same allocation) stated as an obligation; Austral has no named pointer-difference or ordering functions, and its `Pointer` and `Address` types are compared with the ordering operators Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): one trusted `ptr::diff` (same-allocation premise), ordering read from its sign, no `ptr::cmp`; specify the representability of its result, one-past pointers included (Q8 is marked agreed in the picks; ROADMAP records no owner answer for it) | trusted code compares raw pointers with `==` and `<` today (bug 322) | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6, TRUST-1) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): One trusted `ptr::diff`, ordering from its sign, no `ptr::cmp` |
1328: | OD38 | <a id="od38"></a>The root resource API (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD8). Options: `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` with trusted splitting functions (`root.split_alloc()`, `root.split_console()`, ...) returning linear tokens; or a fixed set of token parameters `main` may declare (`fn main(alloc: Alloc, io: Console)`). Recommendation: the single `Root` value, as Austral's `RootCapability`, split by trusted functions the wrapper gate lists Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): `fn main(root: Root) -> u8` (or Unit), `Root` split into linear tokens by trusted functions; specify the split functions and how leftover authority is consumed | `main` takes no parameters today (STMT-7) | R-0557 (TRUST-9) and STMT-7 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: there is no `Root` type; `main`'s parameters are the target's singletons, hosted `fn main() with(...) -> u8` and freestanding `fn main(uart: Uart, arena: Arena) with(Device, Alloc) -> u8`; revised 2026-11-02: `main` receives the target's grants and returns `u8` only ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)) |
1329: | OD39 | <a id="od39"></a>The interface artifact format (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD9). Options: a generated `.coni` text file per module beside the build output; a JSON interface in the build directory; or the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source. Recommendation: rendered Concrete source (declarations without bodies), deterministic and reviewable as a diff Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): the `pub` declarations rendered as Concrete source without bodies, generated by the compiler, with an API-diff gate; it also carries private layout, impl methods, attributes, constants, capabilities and generic bodies, and is not read as FFI-6 bodyless foreign bindings | the compiler has the declaration-level `FileSummary` and `--report interface`, but no persisted per-module interface artifact and no API-diff gate of the proposed form | R-0556 (MOD-7) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `pub` decls rendered as Concrete source, compiler-generated, API-diff gate |
1330: | OD40 | <a id="od40"></a>PAT-9 and the fallback arm of a desugared `if let`, `let ... else` or `while let` (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: the fallback arm is not a written arm and is not judged; or it is judged, so `if let` over a one-variant enum is refused as unreachable. Recommendation: not judged, since the source has no arm to delete Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): moot if OD52 removes the desugared forms | `if let` over a one-variant enum is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Moot: OD52 removed `if let`/`while let`/`let...else` |
1331: | OD41 | <a id="od41"></a>PAT-9 and integer ranges that span their type (added 2026-10-29, while fixing bug 317). Options: a catch-all after ranges covering the whole integer type is not refused (PAT-2 still requires it); or it is refused and PAT-2 is relaxed to accept ranges that cover the type. Recommendation: not refused, keeping PAT-2 as decided Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): close as range-specific under OD56, recording in PAT-2 and PAT-9 that an integer match still needs a catch-all and that listing every literal is not coverage | such a match is accepted | bug 317's repair (PAT-9, PAT-2) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Close as range-specific; integer matches keep a required catch-all, listing every literal is not coverage |
1332: | OD42 | <a id="od42"></a>Comparisons on `bool` and `char` under OD26 (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD26, whose answer says "arithmetic, shifts and comparisons take numbers only"). Two readings: numbers only, so `b == true` and `c < 'z'` are refused (a `bool` is tested directly, a `char` converted by a named function); or "numbers" excludes pointers only, and `bool` and `char` keep equality (and `char` ordering). Recommendation: keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, and refuse only pointers, which is what OD26's alignment with TRUST-1 is about Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): keep `==` and `!=` on `bool` and `char` and ordering on `char`, refuse ordering on `bool`; amend TYPE-6 and OD26 explicitly | the current compiler compares `bool` and `char` | bug 322's repair (TYPE-6) | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `==`/`!=` on bool/char and `<` on char; refuse ordering on bool |
1333: | OD43 | <a id="od43"></a>How a type position resolves a name (added 2026-10-29, from the spec gap bug 229's ruling found). Options: (a) the current compiler's rules: type positions search the type namespace only, a user `enum Option` is always the builtin, and a parent sees its child's newtypes; (b) a user declaration shadows a builtin of the same name, with a warning; (c) a user type may not take a builtin type's name (refused). Recommendation: (c), which removes the shadowing question entirely; Austral reserves its builtin type names the same way Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (c) refused, for builtin-name collisions only, with the complete reserved set published (also for aliases and import renames); this answer would not retire OD43, whose type-versus-value lookup and child-module questions stay open in it. A user `struct Copy HeapArray` is treated as linear today | the current compiler follows (a) | MOD-1; bug 229's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse builtin-name collisions, publish reserved set. Completed 2026-11-02 by the owner: one namespace per module, and a child module's declarations only through `child::T` or an import ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-namespace)) |
1334: | OD44 | <a id="od44"></a>Two packages of one program that define the same top-level module name (added 2026-10-29, from bug 074's ruling). Options: (a) refused when the project loads, naming both packages (the current compiler); (b) module paths are qualified by their package, so both coexist. Recommendation: (a) until packages depend on packages (R-0440), then revisit with (b) Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused at load, naming both packages, until packages depend on packages (R-0440) | the current compiler refuses (bug 074) | MOD-1, MOD-4; R-0440 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse at load, naming both, until R-0440 |
1335: | OD45 | <a id="od45"></a>Capability-variable inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 063's ruling). Main today binds a callee's `cap C` variable from the capability set of an argument's function type when its type peek (`PeekType`) can compute that type without checking the argument, and refuses a variable no argument determines (E0242, `pick_generic::<i64>()`). Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the argument forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) require an explicit capability argument whenever an argument is not a name. Recommendation: (b), so whether a call infers does not depend on the argument's spelling Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) bind from the checked type of every argument; refuse unresolved or inconsistent ones; no result-context inference | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TRUST-4, TYPE-1; bug 063's record | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Bind from checked type of every argument; no result-context. **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked; no capability inference** |
1336: | OD46 | <a id="od46"></a>Generic type-argument inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 308's ruling). Main today binds each type parameter from the types its peek (`PeekType`) computes for the arguments given for it, a value `if` or `match` included since bug 308, but answers `.placeholder` for a generic call's result and some arms with binders, and refuses a parameter no argument determines; it never infers from the expected result type. Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) also infer from the expected type at the call site. Recommendation: (b), local and predictable, with explicit type arguments for the rest; Austral infers type parameters from the arguments, and a `let` declaration's type can disambiguate a return-type-polymorphic call Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) from checked argument types only, never from the expected result; arguments that need an expected type are refused, not defaulted | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TYPE-13, TYPE-15; bug 308's record | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): From checked argument types only |
1337: | OD47 | <a id="od47"></a>Loops: `while` and a counted range loop (added 2026-10-29) | the current compiler has `while` and the C-style `for (init; cond; step)`, `for (cond)` and `for { }`; bug 331 | exit 2q; R-0499; V1a's loops and exits | decided 2026-10-29 by the owner: (1) the C-style `for` is removed in every form; (2) loops are `while cond { ... }` and `for i from a until b { ... }`: half-open (`a`, ..., `b - 1`, empty when `a >= b`), the index immutable in the body, `a` and `b` evaluated once before the first iteration, the index of the bounds' integer type (both bounds of one type), `break` and `continue` as usual (`continue` advances to the next index), `from` and `until` reserved (whether reserved or contextual was reopened by the reviews of that day: OD50), LL(1) as `for` IDENT `from` expr `until` expr block with the restricted head expression for `b` (OD16); (3) in Core the counted loop desugars to the one `while` form, carrying its termination measure `b - i`. Now STMT-6@3, STMT-11@2 and STMT-12 (pending, bug 331) |
1338: | OD48 | <a id="od48"></a>The index type of a counted loop over two literals (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type (write `for i from 0 until n` with a typed bound, or annotate); (b) the index is `Int` there. Recommendation: (a), consistent with OD31 Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) refused, as OD31 refuses any literal with no expected type; a typed bound supplies the other bound's type, in either order | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair (STMT-6) | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused, as OD31 |
1339: | OD49 | <a id="od49"></a>The arithmetic of a counted loop's termination measure (added 2026-10-29, while recording OD47). Options: (a) the measure is a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time; (b) it is computed in the unsigned type of the bounds' width; (c) a counted loop whose `b - a` cannot be represented is refused. Recommendation: (a): the loop compares `i < b` and increments `i`, which cannot overflow below `b`, so the measure need not exist at run time Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) a mathematical integer in the proof only, never computed at run time | the counted loop is not in the current compiler | bug 331's repair; the contracts chapter's loop variant | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Mathematical integer in the proof only |
1340: | OD50 | <a id="od50"></a>Reserved or contextual `from` and `until` (added 2026-10-29, from the reviews of OD47). Options: (a) reserved words everywhere, as OD47 first said: `let from: Int` is refused and the lexer has two more keywords; (b) contextual keywords, meaningful only between `for IDENT` and the block, so existing names stay legal. Recommendation: (a), one lexical rule and no context-dependent tokens, at the cost of two names; Austral reserves its loop words Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) reserved everywhere | the current compiler has neither word (bug 331) | bug 331's repair; SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserved everywhere |
1341: | OD51 | <a id="od51"></a>Reverse, stepped and collection iteration (added 2026-10-29, from the reviews of OD47). Options: (a) none: the counted loop is ascending by one, everything else is a `while`; (b) add a descending form (`for i from a down until b`) and a step (`by k`) later, each its own decision; (c) add iteration over a collection. Recommendation: (a) now, revisiting (b) when a workload needs it; a `while` keeps the proof story at one loop construct Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (a) none now; each a later decision when a workload needs it | the current compiler has no counted loop (bug 331) | STMT-6 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): None now |
1342: | OD52 | <a id="od52"></a>`if let`, `while let` and `let ... else` (added 2026-10-30, item S1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `if let`, `while let` and `let ... else` stay in the language or are removed in favour of `match`. Options: (a) keep the three forms (PAT-6, their desugaring to `match`); (b) remove them, `match` being the only refutable pattern form. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: none of the three; `case` is the only refutable form | all three are accepted and desugar to a `match` with a catch-all arm (PAT-6) | PAT-6, PAT-8, PAT-9, OPEN-40, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1343: | OD53 | <a id="od53"></a>Struct update `..base` (added 2026-10-30, item S2 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the struct update form `S { a: 1, ..base }` stays in the language. Options: (a) keep it; (b) remove it, every field written. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (b) remove. Austral: no struct update; every field is written | accepted (TYPE-7, OWN-15's value flow for `..base`) | TYPE-7, OWN-15 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |

---
1373: | OD83 | <a id="od83"></a>The call-site allocator binding in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C6 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains the call-site allocator binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: not checked | the parser accepts it | TRUST-4, MEM-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No call-site binding `f(...) with(Alloc = e)`. Under the authority decision, one allocator form per target: hosted, `with(Alloc)` and no allocator value; freestanding, an `Arena` parameter plus `with(Alloc)`. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: one form on every target, an `Allocator<A>` value plus `with(Alloc)` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-allocation)) |
1374: | OD84 | <a id="od84"></a>Struct destructuring `let` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C7 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains struct destructuring `let S { a } = e;`. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): individual review required; no pick recorded. Austral: destructuring `let` of records exists (not checked) | the parser accepts it (PAT-5) | PAT-5, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Add struct-destructuring `let` |
1375: | OD85 | <a id="od85"></a>Variant destructuring with `else` in the grammar (added 2026-10-30, item C8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar gains `let E::V { a } = e else { ... };`, which OD52's pick would remove. Options: (a) add it to the grammar; (b) remove it from the parser, so the language loses the form; (c) keep the recorded divergence for now. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): follows OD52 (removed under its pick). Austral: no `let ... else` | the parser accepts it (PAT-8) | PAT-8, OPEN-52, SYN-1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove (follows OD52) |
1376: | OD86 | <a id="od86"></a>The body of an unbraced match arm (added 2026-10-30, item R1 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar narrows an unbraced match-arm body to an expression, a block or `return`, which is what the parser accepts (`continue;` as an unbraced body is refused). Options: (a) narrow the grammar; (b) widen the parser. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) narrow. Austral: not applicable | the parser accepts an expression, a block or `return` as an unbraced arm body and refuses other statements | SYN-1, PAT-3 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Narrow |
1377: | OD87 | <a id="od87"></a>Capability arguments in an expression turbofish (added 2026-10-30, item R2/R3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether capability arguments (`T + C`, `{}`) may be written in an expression or method turbofish. Options: (a) the grammar narrows to type arguments there until a workload needs them; (b) the parser adds them. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) narrow. Austral: not applicable | the parser refuses them; the grammar derives them | SYN-1, TRUST-4, OPEN-45 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept explicit capability arguments in a turbofish (inference fills omitted ones). **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): explicit capability arguments are required; inference no longer fills omitted ones** |
1378: | OD88 | <a id="od88"></a>The shape of capability arguments (added 2026-10-30, item R8 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether the grammar states the capability-argument shape rules the parser checks (a union of names only; type arguments before capability arguments). Options: (a) state them in the grammar; (b) leave them to the parser. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a). Austral: not applicable | the parser checks them; the grammar does not | SYN-1, TRUST-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State in grammar |
1379: | OD89 | <a id="od89"></a>Capability parameters on a trait impl (added 2026-10-30, item R9 of the parser/grammar divergence report of 2026-10). Question: Whether a trait impl may declare capability parameters, which the grammar derives and the parser refuses. Options: (a) refused, the grammar narrowed; (b) allowed. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the divergence report, not the picks). Austral: not applicable | the parser refuses them (declare them on an inherent impl) | SYN-1, TRAIT-9, TRUST-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse; declare on inherent impl |
1380: | OD90 | <a id="od90"></a>`struct Copy <T>` and contextual keywords (added 2026-10-30, item Copy of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether `Copy` stays a contextual keyword, with the LL(1) checkers taught to model it, or becomes a reserved word (the other unreserved words, `self`, `repr`, `packed` and `align`, stay as they are unless decided separately). Options: (a) reserve `Copy` as a keyword, refusing it as an identifier; (b) keep it contextual, the LL(1) checkers taught to model a quoted unreserved terminal as also IDENT, each conflict found listed for the owner (letters as in the divergence report). Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) reserve `Copy`: one lexical rule, consistent with OD50 (reserved `from` and `until`) and OD43 (builtin names refused as user names). Austral: its keywords are reserved | contextual; the `struct Copy <T>` conflict is invisible to the checkers' token model | SYN-1, OWN-D1 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Reserve `Copy` |
1381: | OD91 | <a id="od91"></a>A cast followed by `<` (added 2026-10-30, item LL(1) type\|5.2 of grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv). Question: How `x as T < y` parses, given that `<` after a cast's type name may start type arguments or be the less-than operator. Options: (a) a cast's type takes no type arguments unless parenthesized (`x as (T<U>)`), so `<` is less-than; (b) the parser's reading: `<` always starts type arguments, so the comparison needs parentheses (`(x as T) < y`); (c) refuse `<` directly after a cast's type and require parentheses either way. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): none given (in the LL(1) ledger, not the picks). Austral: a conversion is the cast expression `e : T`, which takes no type arguments after it, so the conflict does not arise | the parser always reads type arguments, so `x as T < y` is refused (grammar/ll1_known_conflicts.tsv, row type\|5.2) | SYN-1, SYN-2, ARITH-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `x as T < y` is less-than; a generic cast type needs parentheses `x as (Vec<T>)` |
1382: | OD92 | <a id="od92"></a>A capability set inferred from callbacks (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a combinator may write its capability set as that of its callbacks (`with(f)`), the checker computing the union of the sets of the callbacks its body calls. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no such form; `with(f)` reads `f` as a capability name (an unresolved one, which MOD-1 refuses and the current compiler accepts: bug 337), so a combinator names a capability parameter (`fn map<T, U, cap C>(.., f: fn(&T) with(C) -> U) with(C)`). The callable-values design (2026-06-12, [CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md) §6) recorded the shortcut as specified and not built, after [research/language/capability-polymorphism.md](../legacy/research/language/capability-polymorphism.md); no roadmap item tracks it. Options: (a) no shortcut, combinators name capability parameters; (b) add it, as set propagation and not row unification. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-92 | see OPEN-92 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No shortcut; name a capability parameter |
1383: | OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
1384: | OD94 | <a id="od94"></a>Names in `with(...)` that are not capabilities (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a name in `with(...)` that is not a capability, a capability alias or a capability variable in scope is refused. Bears on TRUST-D1, TYPE-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: accepted as a capability of its own: `fn main() with(Foo) -> Int` compiles and runs, and a call to a function that requires `Foo` is refused only in a caller that does not l
ist it (E0520). The compiler's `validCaps`, TRUST-4's rationale and [SAFETY.md](../legacy/docs/verification/SAFETY.md) name nine capabilities. Options: (a) refuse an unknown name; (b) an open vocabulary. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | [MOD-1](../legacy/docs/spec/modules.md#MOD-1) refuses the name; the current compiler accepts it (bug 337) | MOD-1@1 | withdrawn 2026-10-30: not an owner question; MOD-1@1 refuses the name (bug 337) |
1385: | OD95 | <a id="od95"></a>What `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` mean (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: What authority each of `Time`, `Env`, `Process` and `Alloc` names. Bears on TRUST-D1, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no document states them; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §2 defined `Console`, `File` and `Network`, and SAFETY.md `Random` (TRUST-D1). std requires `Time` for its clock and sleep functions, `Env` for environment variables and for `std.net`'s `bind` and `connect`, `Process` for exit, spawn, wait and kill, and `Alloc` for heap allocation. Options: state each as std uses it, or narrow or split it (std's `Env` on socket functions is the case to settle). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-95 | see OPEN-95 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): State each; move socket `bind`/`connect` from `Env` to `Network` |
1386: | OD96 | <a id="od96"></a>Two foreign bindings of one C symbol (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether two foreign bindings of one C symbol (`#[link_name]`) must agree on its C signature. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-5. The current compiler: accepted: `abs_a(x: i32) with() -> i32` and `abs_b(x: i64) with() -> i64`, both bound to `abs`, compile and the binary runs, while the interpreter stops at run time on the mismatched width. HANDLE_CAPABILITIES §5 (encoding A, one binding per effect for one C symbol) planned to refuse a mismatch with a diagnostic. Options: (a) refuse; (b) allow, each binding's signature trusted on its own. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-96 | see OPEN-96 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse |

---
1395: | OD105 | <a id="od105"></a>Where a counted loop's `#[invariant]` is checked (added 2026-10-30, from the same review). Question: where an `#[invariant]` on a counted loop is checked, given that CON-2 checks a loop invariant before the loop and after every iteration. Options: (a) as CON-2 says for every loop; (b) at each entry to the body, so nothing is checked when the loop runs no iteration. No pick given; (b) needs proof requirement 2 | the counted loop is not in the current compiler (bug 331) | STMT-12, CON-2 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): As CON-2: before the loop and after every iteration |
1396: | OD106 | <a id="od106"></a>The result type of a checked conversion (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a checked conversion (`try_convert`) reports a value it cannot preserve as `Option::None` or as a `Result` error carrying the reason. Bears on ARITH-5, ARITH-10. The current compiler: no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 wrote `try_convert::<T>(x) -> Option<T>` "(or a `Result` with a reason)" and left the choice to the API's owner. Options: (a) `Option<T>`; (b) a `Result` whose error names the reason (out of range, not integral, NaN, infinite, not representable). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | no checked conversion exists (E0101, R-0500) | ARITH-5, ARITH-10 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `Result<T, OutOfRange>` (failure, not absence) |
1397: | OD107 | <a id="od107"></a>What a float-to-integer rounding gives for NaN, infinities and out-of-range values (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether each float-to-integer rounding operation (`trunc`, `round`, `floor`, `ceil`) gives a value or an error for NaN, an infinity or a result outside the target type's range, and which value. Bears on ARITH-1, ARITH-11. The current compiler: the rounding operations do not exist (R-0500); a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range, natively and in `--interp`. The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) §8.2 required a stated result for every input, "either as a value or as an error". Options: (a) an error, as `try_convert` reports; (b) a value: the nearest value of the type for an out-of-range input or an infinity, and 0 for NaN; (c) a trap, as `as` does today. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the rounding operations do not exist; a float-to-integer `as` truncates toward zero and aborts on NaN, an infinity or a value out of range | ARITH-1, ARITH-11 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): An error, as `try_convert` |
1398: | OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
1399: | OD109 | <a id="od109"></a>Specializations that differ only in a function type's capabilities (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether the symbol of a generic function's instantiation includes the capability set of a function-type argument, so that two instantiations that differ only in that set have distinct symbols. Bears on TYPE-17, TYPE-18. The current compiler: one: `pick<T: Copy>` instantiated at `fn(Int) -> Int` and at `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` emits the single function `pick_for_Fn_T_Int_to_Int_E`, because Mono's `tyToSuffix` drops a function type's capability set while it keeps a struct's capability arguments (E0809's history), and the program runs correctly since capabilities have no run-time representation (TYPE-18). The archived [HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/HANDLE_CAPABILITIES.md) R8 required collision-free symbol identity across instantiations and left code sharing to its own argument. Options: (a) the set is part of the symbol, as for struct capability arguments; (b) it is not, one symbol serving both, stated as erasure with its correctness argument. Whether two symbols share one body stays an implementation choice (TYPE-18). Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | one specialization: Mono's symbol drops a function type's capability set | TYPE-17, TYPE-18 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Set is part of the symbol |
1400: | OD110 | <a id="od110"></a>Enums at the foreign boundary (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value of enum type may cross an `extern fn` boundary, and if so in what representation. Bears on FFI-3, FFI-7. The current compiler: refused: `extern fn f(x: E)` with `enum Copy E { A, B }` is E0574 (a non-FFI-safe parameter); only scalars, raw pointers and `repr(C)` structs cross. The design record [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) says enums must not cross (§3.2); before it was archived it also said they cross as integers. Options: (a) refused, as in the current compiler; (b) a fieldless enum crosses as an integer of a stated width, which needs a stated discriminant for each variant. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | refused (E0574) | FFI-3, FFI-7 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refused |
1401: | OD111 | <a id="od111"></a>Misaligned fields of a packed struct (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a `#[repr(packed)]` struct may hold a field whose alignment exceeds the alignment of its offset, and whether such a field may be borrowed. Bears on FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5. The current compiler: both accepted: `#[repr(packed)] struct P { v: u8, l: u32 }` places `l` at offset 1, and `&p.l` is accepted and reads the field correctly in the binary and in `--interp`. [LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/LAYOUT_CONTRACT.md) §2 restricts a packed struct to fields whose alignment does not exceed their position, without saying what enforces it. Options: (a) refuse such a field; (b) accept it and refuse a reference to it; (c) accept both, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted on both consumers | FFI-4, FFI-8, REF-5 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept field, refuse a reference to it |
1402: | OD112 | <a id="od112"></a>Mutation through a shared reference (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a value reached through a shared reference `&T` may be changed, by a write through a raw pointer derived from the reference or by a cell type. Bears on REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5. The current compiler: safe code changes a value only through a `mut` binding or a `&mut` reference, and there is no cell type; a trusted function may convert `r: &Int` to `*mut Int` (`r as *const Int as *mut Int`) and store through it: the binary does the store, and `--interp` stops ("deref-assign through immutable ref"). The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §12 said there is no `Cell`, `RefCell` or `UnsafeCell` equivalent and that mutation flows from `let mut` through `&mut`; the [overview](../legacy/docs/spec/overview.md) lists no interior mutability in safe code, with a later `UnsafeCell<T>` gated by `Unsafe`. Options: (a) never: a write through a pointer derived from a `&T` is undefined behaviour, a premise trusted code keeps (TRUST-2); (b) as (a), with a cell type gated by `Unsafe` as the one exception; (c) a trusted write through such a pointer is allowed. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | safe code cannot; a trusted write through `r as *const T as *mut T` runs in the binary and stops `--interp` | REF-10, OWN-10, TRUST-2, TRUST-5 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Never; UB premise trusted code keeps |
1403: | OD113 | <a id="od113"></a>The ownership exceptions of trusted code (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether trusted code keeps its two exceptions to the ownership rules, destroying a value through a reference and releasing with `vec_free` a vector whose elements are not Copy. Bears on TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8. The current compiler: both are accepted in trusted code only (elsewhere E0290 under OWN-8 and E0287 under OWN-5): std's collections destroy an element through `(p as &T)` in drop glue after its owner was consumed, and a trusted function may free a buffer it has emptied. The archived [MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/MEMORY_SEMANTICS.md) §11 said that `trusted` does not relax linearity, and TRUST-5's rationale says the same. Options: (a) keep both, each a named trusted operation with its premise (the owner was consumed; the vector is empty); (b) remove both, drop glue moving the element out of raw memory instead (TRUST-7); (c) keep only destruction through a reference; (d) keep only `vec_free` of an emptied vector. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | both accepted in trusted code only (E0290, E0287 elsewhere) | TRUST-5, TRUST-12, OWN-5, OWN-8 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove both |
1404: | OD114 | <a id="od114"></a>How integer division rounds (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: How an integer division that does not abort rounds its quotient, and which sign a nonzero remainder takes. Bears on ARITH-3. The current compiler: the quotient is truncated toward zero and a nonzero remainder has the sign of the dividend, as C99 and LLVM's `sdiv` and `srem` give (`-7 / 2` is `-3`, `-7 % 2` is `-1`, `7 % -2` is `1`), in the binary, the interpreter and the constant folder (`IntArith.evalIntBinOp`). The archived [ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/ARITHMETIC_POLICY.md) states when division aborts (§5) but not how it rounds. Options: (a) truncation toward zero, as in the current compiler; (b) floored division, the remainder taking the divisor's sign; (c) Euclidean division, the remainder never negative. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | the quotient is truncated toward zero and a remainder has the dividend's sign (C99) | ARITH-3 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Truncation toward zero |
1405: | OD115 | <a id="od115"></a>The body and totality of a specification function (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a `spec fn` may be declared without a body, and whether its body must be shown total. Bears on CON-3, CON-5, CON-1. The current compiler: both `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool;` and `spec fn big(x: Int) -> bool = x > 10;` are accepted, and so is a recursive body that never terminates (`spec fn loopy(x: Int) -> bool = loopy(x + 1);`), with no check. [CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_GUIDE.md) calls a `spec fn` "an erased mathematical specification, no body"; contracts.md's examples (CON-3) give it a body; [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) requires it to be total. Options: (a) a body is required and must be total (structural recursion or a checked measure); (b) a body is optional, a body-less one an uninterpreted function a proof must constrain, and a body must be total; (c) as in the current compiler, no totality check. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-115 | see OPEN-115 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Body required and total |
1406: | OD116 | <a id="od116"></a>A checked total function category (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether the language has a `total fn` category, a runtime function checked to terminate without trapping that contracts may then call. Bears on CON-1, CON-3, CON-5. The current compiler: there is no such category (`total fn f(..)` is a parse error, E0001); a contract clause may still call a capability-free runtime function until CON-3's judgment lands. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Design Principles) distinguishes `spec fn`, a checked `total fn`, ordinary and trusted functions, and admits a `total fn` into contracts once its obligations close. Options: (a) add the category; (b) no category: contracts call only `spec fn` (CON-3's full judgment). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-116 | see OPEN-116 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No category |
1407: | OD117 | <a id="od117"></a>`old`, quantifiers and implication in contracts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether contract expressions gain `old(x)`, quantifiers and an implication or equivalence operator, and with what syntax. Bears on CON-2, CON-3, SYN-1. The current compiler: `#[ensures(result == old(x))]` compiles and runs, and `--report contracts` marks the clause `invalid_contract_expression` (unknown function `old`); `forall i: Int, ...` is a parse error (E0001); there is no implication operator. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Contract Expression V1) lists all three as deferred. Options: (a) add each, with a syntax decided per form; (b) keep them out, a claim needing one being a Lean theorem; (c) refuse `old(..)` now as an unknown name, deciding the rest later. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-117 | see OPEN-117 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No `old`, quantifiers or implication in contracts; state such properties as Lean theorems |
1408: | OD118 | <a id="od118"></a>A proven run-time violation in trusted code (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether an index the compiler proves out of bounds, or a division it proves to be by zero, is refused in `trusted` and `with(Unsafe)` code as in safe code. Bears on MEM-6, ARITH-3, TRUST-5, TRUST-12. The current compiler: `a[5]` on a `[Int; 3]`, or `x / 0`, is refused in safe code (E0900, `error[runtime-safety]`) and compiles in a `trusted fn` (the index also in a function declaring `Unsafe`), where it aborts at run time on both consumers. MEM-6 states the refusal with no exemption; [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) C7 records the exemption as intended. Options: (a) refuse everywhere, as MEM-6 reads; (b) keep the exemption and state it in MEM-6. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-118 | see OPEN-118 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse everywhere **Subsumed 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): refusal of proven violations becomes refusal of unproven safety, everywhere.** |
1409: | OD119 | <a id="od119"></a>Buffered output when the process aborts (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether output the program wrote before a trap or abort is flushed before the process ends. Bears on STMT-1, STMT-2, PASS-13, OPEN-101. The current compiler: print builtins write through C's buffered stdio, and `abort` does not flush it on glibc (a binary that prints `hello` and then traps prints nothing on Linux) while it does on Darwin; the interpreter models the same buffer and the same platform difference. STMT-2 says a failure ends the process at once and is silent on output already written, so the observable trace of a trapping program differs by platform. Recorded by [INTERPRETER_TRUST.md](../legacy/docs/verification/INTERPRETER_TRUST.md) §2. Options: (a) everything written before the trap is output (flush on abort, or unbuffered output); (b) what was written is unspecified on a trap, and PASS-13 compares output only for exiting runs. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-119 | see OPEN-119 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Everything written before the trap is output |
1410: | OD120 | <a id="od120"></a>A vacuous contract (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a contract whose precondition is unsatisfiable is refused by the language or only reported, under a project policy. Bears on CON-1, CON-2. The current compiler: `#[requires(x > 5 && x < 3)]` compiles; a decided-unsatisfiable precondition makes the claim `vacuous`, never `proved`, and E0613 refuses it only under the `forbid-vacuous` policy (`Concrete.Policy`), not in a plain build. [CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md](../legacy/docs/verification/CONTRACTS_AND_VCS.md) (Soundness Bridge) states the reporting rule. Options: (a) a language refusal in every build; (b) tooling only: always reported `vacuous`, refused under a policy, as in the current compiler. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-120 | see OPEN-120 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Tooling: reported `vacuous`, refused under policy **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a vacuous precondition used for safety admission is refused in every build.** |
1411: | OD121 | <a id="od121"></a>Projections from a base that is not a traceable place (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the verification documents). Question: Whether a field access, index or `*` whose base is not a traceable place (an `if` or `match` value, `**r`, a reference read out of storage) is refused, or accepted once the checker can trace it. Bears on REF-5, MEM-4, MEM-8, TYPE-7. The current compiler: refused (E0238, `untraceablePlace`), whether the projection reads a Copy value or forms a reference: `(if c { a } else { b }).f`, `(**r).f`, `*w.r`. No entry states the refusal: REF-5 forbids a branch value or a stored reference as a reference's root, and MEM-4, MEM-8 and TYPE-7 do not restrict a projection's base. [KNOWN_HOLES.md](../legacy/docs/verification/KNOWN_HOLES.md) H28 calls it a deliberate restriction until R-0499's checker on Core. Options: (a) state the restriction as a rule (a projection's base is a place: a binding, its fields and elements, or `*` of a named reference); (b) accept such projections of owned values and Copy reads once the checker traces them, refusing only what REF-5 refuses. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/verification, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-121 | see OPEN-121 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): State the restriction as a rule |

---
1413: | OD123 | <a id="od123"></a>`as` on a newtype outside its module (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether `v as N` and `n as T`, between a newtype `N` and its inner type `T`, are accepted outside the module that declares `N`, where TYPE-11 refuses `N(v)`. Bears on TYPE-11, TYPE-24, ARITH-5, OPEN-54. The current compiler: both accepted in any module, so `x as Id` builds a foreign newtype that `Id(x)` may not (E0296), and a validated wrapper's smart constructor can be bypassed. [VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/VALIDATED_WRAPPERS.md) §8 describes the cast rule as guarding the wrapper contract from an `as` bypass, which holds for casts to unrelated types only. Options: (a) accepted anywhere, as in the current compiler; (b) accepted only where `N(v)` and `.0` are (TYPE-11, with R-0500's follow-on for `.0`); (c) `as` never names a newtype, the conversions being `N(v)` and `.0`. Austral: no newtype (OPEN-54's comparison). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-123 | see OPEN-123 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `as` never names a newtype |
1414: | OD124 | <a id="od124"></a>Profiles in the specification (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether the profiles (the admission profiles Safe, Predictable and Proof, and the build profiles) belong to the language specification, in a chapter of their own, and whether a profile may change what an accepted program does. Bears on ARITH-9, OPEN-97, PASS-3, PASS-4. The current compiler: a profile only refuses or reports: `--check predictable` reports its five gates (no recursion, bounded loops, no allocation, no extern calls, no `File`, `Network` or `Process`), `[policy] predictable = true` in `Concrete.toml` turns a violation into E0610, and no profile changes the behaviour of a program it accepts; ARITH-9 states that for arithmetic. [PROFILES.md](../legacy/docs/platform/PROFILES.md) says a profile never changes what a program means, while OPEN-97 asks whether a build profile may omit a run-time check. Options: (a) no profiles chapter: profiles are tooling, and the spec states only that no profile changes an accepted program's behaviour; (b) a profiles chapter whose entries state each profile's admission rules as refusals, with that same guarantee; (c) as (b), with build profiles allowed to differ as OPEN-97 decides. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-124 | see OPEN-124 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Profiles chapter of refusals; no behaviour change |
1415: | OD125 | <a id="od125"></a>Contracts at run time (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether a `#[requires]`, `#[ensures]` or loop `#[invariant]` that no proof discharges is checked at run time, trapping when it fails, or is only a claim. Bears on CON-1, CON-2, STMT-2. The current compiler: contracts are erased metadata: a call that violates `#[requires(x > 0)]` runs normally, in the binary and in `--interp`, and `assert` elaborates to proof evidence only (syntax chapter introduction). [ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/ERROR_CONVENTIONS.md) lists contract-violation traps among the fatal failures. Options: (a) claims only, never checked at run time, as in the current compiler; (b) checked at run time and trapping unless discharged; (c) checked under a build profile only (OPEN-97, OPEN-124). Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-125 | see OPEN-125 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Claims only **Qualified 2026-11-06 by [no hidden traps](#decided-project-no-hidden-traps): a claim used for safety admission needs closed evidence.** |
1416: | OD126 | <a id="od126"></a>Target assumptions (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Which target facts are language rules: 64-bit pointers, little-endian byte order, POSIX hosts, and the sizes of `bool`, `Unit` and pointers. Bears on FFI-2, FFI-7, TYPE-19, TYPE-20. The current compiler: one target profile: `Layout` hardcodes 8-byte pointers and references, `bool` of 1 byte, `Unit` of 0 bytes, an `i32` enum tag, and assumes little-endian POSIX hosts; FFI-2 names AAPCS64 for the native profile, and the integer types' widths follow from their names (TYPE-19). [EXECUTION_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/EXECUTION_MODEL.md) and [ABI.md](../legacy/docs/platform/ABI.md) state these as platform facts. Options: (a) language rules, a 32-bit or big-endian target being another language profile; (b) target facts, observable only through `sizeof`, `alignof` (FFI-7) and named target constants; (c) as (b), with the sizes of `bool` and `Unit` fixed by the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-126 | see OPEN-126 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Language rules; other targets are other profiles. Generalized 2026-11-08 by the [foundation decision](#foundation-q3): profiles are read-only and separate address width, pointer representation, length width, byte order and address spaces; the existing 64-bit little-endian profile and its `i32` enum tags are unchanged |
1417: | OD127 | <a id="od127"></a>UTF-8 in every `String` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the platform and stdlib documents). Question: Whether every `String` value that safe code can reach holds valid UTF-8, as a language guarantee. Bears on TYPE-3, SYN-5, TRUST-2. The current compiler: literals are UTF-8 (SYN-5), but nothing keeps a `String` valid: `push_char` appends a byte unchecked, `Bytes::to_string` transfers bytes without validation, and std's `read_to_string` does not validate. [STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/STRING_TEXT_CONTRACT.md) §1 states the invariant as a target, and [UNICODE_POLICY.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/UNICODE_POLICY.md) has a validating `Bytes::to_string` returning `Option`. Options: (a) a language guarantee, every safe constructor validating and an unchecked one being a trusted operation with that premise; (b) a std convention only, `String` being bytes to the language. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/platform and docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-127 | see OPEN-127 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Language guarantee |
1418: | OD128 | <a id="od128"></a>`->` between a match arm's pattern and body (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a match arm may separate its pattern from its body with `->` as well as `=>`. Bears on SYN-1 and PAT-7. The current compiler: the parser accepts both (`parseMatchArms`; `2 -> { return 42; }` exits 42 on both consumers), and the grammar's `match_arm` has only `=>`. The syntax freeze review (2026-04, [SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/SYNTAX_FREEZE_REVIEW.md) §1.5) recommended `=>` as the canonical form with a warning for `->`, which also spells a return type. Options: (a) `=>` only, `->` refused; (b) both accepted; (c) both accepted, with a warning on `->`, as the freeze review recommended. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-128 | see OPEN-128 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): `=>` only |
1419: | OD129 | <a id="od129"></a>Type arguments of an enum literal taken from its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an enum literal or variant written without type arguments (`Option::Some { value: 42 }`, `Option::None`) takes them from the type its position expects, and from which positions. Bears on TYPE-7, TYPE-15 and OPEN-46, which asks the same of a generic call's type arguments and whose pick excludes the expected result type. The current compiler: the type arguments come from the expected type of a `return` (the function's result type), of an annotated `let` and of a call argument (the parameter's type); all three exit 42 on both consumers, and spec examples rely on it (PAT-1). The syntax freeze review (§1.3, kill-list items 1, 4 and 5) proposed this inference for enum literals and for constructor calls such as `Vec::new()`. Options: (a) explicit type arguments always; (b) from the expected type in the positions the current compiler uses; (c) the answer to OPEN-46, applied to enum literals. Austral: type parameters are inferred from the arguments, and a declaration's type can disambiguate. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-129 | see OPEN-129 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Same rule as OD46: type arguments from the arguments, otherwise explicit (`None::<i32>`) |
1420: | OD130 | <a id="od130"></a>Unlabeled pattern binders: by position or by name (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether an unlabeled binder in a struct or variant pattern (`E::V { y, x }`) binds the field of its own name or the field at its position. Bears on PAT-1, PAT-3 and PAT-10. The current compiler disagrees with itself: with `enum E { V { x: Int, y: Int } }` and `E::V { x: 40, y: 2 }`, the arm `E::V { y, x } => y` gives 40 in `match` and in `if let` (binders zip the variant's fields in declaration order, `Concrete/Elab/Elab.lean` `bindings.zip ev.fields`), while the struct destructuring `let S { y, x } = s; y` gives 2 (by name); both consumers agree. PAT-1's `field: binder` form and the grammar's `binding_list` comment ("positional") point different ways. Options: (a) by name (field punning) everywhere; (b) by position everywhere; (c) refuse an unlabeled list whose order differs from the declaration. Austral: not applicable (no punning). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the variant-pattern disagreement may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-130 | see OPEN-130 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): By name everywhere |
1421: | OD131 | <a id="od131"></a>`union` declarations (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether the language has `union` declarations, and if so what reading a field other than the one last written means. Bears on SYN-1, TYPE-7, FFI-7 and FFI-8. The current compiler: `union U { a: Int, b: Uint }` parses (`Concrete/Frontend/Parser.lean`, as a struct whose fields share storage) and compiles; a value built as `U { a: 42 }` is linear, and `union` is neither a keyword nor in the grammar. No document specifies it. Options: (a) remove it; (b) keep it, for the foreign boundary only, with its layout and read rules specified. Austral: no unions. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-131 | see OPEN-131 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1422: | OD132 | <a id="od132"></a>Polymorphic recursion (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a generic function that calls itself at a strictly larger type argument (`f::<Box<T>>` inside `f<T>`) is refused, and by which check. Bears on TYPE-13, PASS-7 and PASS-12. The current compiler: such a program is not refused; compilation does not terminate (more than 200 s and 2 GB on 2026-10-30, natively and with `--interp`), since monomorphization specializes without a bound on function instances (the bound `closeInstances` has covers only generic type definitions). [PASSES.md](../legacy/docs/compiler/PASSES.md) §7 lists unbounded polymorphic recursion as Mono's error. Options: (a) refuse a call cycle whose type arguments grow, before Mono; (b) refuse at Mono when specialization exceeds a stated limit. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30; the non-termination may also be filed as a bug). | see OPEN-132 | see OPEN-132 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse growing call cycles before Mono |
1423: | OD133 | <a id="od133"></a>User functions named `print`, `println` or `append` (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing the compiler documents and the grammar comments). Question: Whether a program may declare a function named `print`, `println` or `append`, which then replaces the builtin of that name in calls the program writes. Bears on MOD-3, MOD-8 and TYPE-10. The current compiler: accepted; with `fn print(x: Int) -> Int`, `print(42)` calls it (exit 42 on both consumers), because Elab desugars the variadic builtins only when no function of that name is visible, and MOD-3's reserved names do not include them. MOD-8 already binds the calls the compiler itself generates by identity. Options: (a) reserve the three names (MOD-3); (b) allow the replacement, as the current compiler does. Austral: not applicable. Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-133 | see OPEN-133 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Reserve the three names |
1424: | OD134 | <a id="od134"></a>The capabilities of a container's generated destruction (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/stdlib). Question: Whether the destruction a generic container generates for its elements may require the capabilities of those elements' `Destroy` implementations beyond `Alloc`, or every `Destroy` implementation stays limited to `Alloc`. Bears on OWN-13, TYPE-D1 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: OWN-13 is enforced, so a `Destroy` implementation requires no capability beyond `Alloc`, and a container's generated destruction is `with(Alloc)`. [RUNTIME_COLLECTIONS.md](../legacy/docs/stdlib/RUNTIME_COLLECTIONS.md) rule 3 describes a different model, in which the generated destruction requires the union of its element, key and value destructors' capabilities, computed per instantiation, and calls today's limit a first-release restriction. Options: (a) keep OWN-13's limit, so destruction never needs more than `Alloc`; (b) lift it, so a `Destroy` implementation may require further capabilities and a container's destruction requires their union per instantiation. Austral: not assessed. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/stdlib, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-134 | see OPEN-134 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Keep `Alloc` limit |
1425: | OD135 | <a id="od135"></a>A method's `Type_method` name in its module's namespace (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether an impl method `m` of a type `T` also occupies the name `T_m` in its module, so that a free function of that name is refused. Bears on MOD-3 and MOD-8. The current compiler: refused: `fn P_get(a: Int) -> Int` beside `impl P { fn get(&self) -> Int }` is E0299 under MOD-3@2 ("a function named 'P_get' is also declared in this module as an impl method"), on both consumers, because the method is entered under its mangled name. MOD-3's reserved names are the `__` prefix, `user_main` and the builtins, which do not include such names. Options: (a) keep it, listing `T_m` among the names MOD-3 reserves; (b) stop entering methods under `T_m`, so the free function is accepted. Austral: not applicable (no methods). Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/compiler, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-135 | see OPEN-135 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Stop entering methods under `T_m` |
1426: | OD136 | <a id="od136"></a>std's `process_exit` (added 2026-10-30, raised while reviewing the absorption of docs/compiler and docs/platform). Question: Whether std keeps `process_exit`, which ends the process through libc `exit` without running any deferred call or consuming the caller's owned values. Bears on STMT-7, OWN-2 and TRUST-4. The current compiler: `std.process.process_exit(code: i32) with(Process)` exists and calls libc `exit`; the language itself has no exit operation (STMT-7). The design record [MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md](../legacy/docs/platform/MAIN_EXIT_MODEL.md) calls an exit operation deliberately absent, since in a linear language it would bypass the proof that every owned value is consumed. Options: (a) keep it, stating that it skips cleanup and that `Process` is what grants it; (b) remove it, status flowing back through `main`'s return only. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the review of the absorption of docs/platform, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-136 | see OPEN-136 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Remove |
1427: | OD137 | <a id="od137"></a>Character literals outside ASCII (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which byte a char literal denotes when its character is not ASCII. Bears on SYN-6, TYPE-20 and SYN-5. The current compiler: the literal silently takes the low byte of the character's code point: `'é'` (U+00E9) is 233, `'ā'` (U+0101) is 1 and `'€'` (U+20AC) is 172 as `u8`, on both consumers, and SYN-6 says only that a char literal is a token. Options: (a) refuse a char literal whose character is not ASCII, so every accepted literal is the one byte of its ASCII character; (b) accept U+0000 to U+00FF as that byte (Latin-1) and refuse anything above; (c) make `char` a Unicode scalar value, which changes TYPE-20's one-byte `char`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-137 | see OPEN-137 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Refuse non-ASCII |
1428: | OD138 | <a id="od138"></a>Which positions give a literal its expected type (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Which positions give an integer literal operand its expected type under ARITH-2@3 (and a float literal's, should OPEN-63 give floats the same rule). Bears on ARITH-2, TYPE-4 and TYPE-6. The current compiler: an unconstrained integer literal is typed `Int` and a float literal `Float64` (bug 327); with `x: u8`, a literal beside `x` takes `u8` as the other operand of `+`, `<` and `&` and as the count of `>>`: `x + 300`, `x < 300`, `x & 300` and `x >> 300` are each refused natively with E0227 (`integer literal 300 is out of range for type 'u8'`), and `300 as Int` is accepted, whether through `as` or the `Int` default the probe does not show. ARITH-2@3 refuses an integer literal with no expected type but does not list the positions that supply one. Options: (a) declared positions (an annotated binding, a parameter, a return, a field) and the other operand of a binary operator, as the probe shows the current compiler doing for `+`, `<`, `&` and a shift count (other operators were not probed); (b) declared positions only, so a literal beside a typed operand needs a typed binding; (c) option (a), plus the target type of an `as` for its literal operand. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-138 | see OPEN-138 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Declared positions + other operand of a binary op |
1429: | OD139 | <a id="od139"></a>A qualified call to a function that is not imported (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether `a::f()` may call a `pub` function of another module that the caller has not imported. Bears on MOD-2, SYN-10 and MOD-4. The current compiler: accepted: `mod a { pub fn answer() -> u8 { return 42; } }` and `a::answer()` in `main` exits 42 with no import, as MOD-4's and SYN-10's accepted examples also do, while MOD-2's sentence says another module uses a declaration only by importing it. Options: (a) a qualified path to a `pub` declaration is a use in its own right, and MOD-2 says so; (b) an import is required, and the unimported qualified call is refused. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-139 | see OPEN-139 | decided 2026-10-31 by the owner's rule (every reviewer agreed, as strict as Austral or stricter): Import required |
1430: | OD140 | <a id="od140"></a>Implicitly dropped results of `free` and of a deferred call (added 2026-10-30, raised by the spec quality audit of 2026-10-30). Question: Whether the result of `free(h)` (the pointee, of type `T`) when `T` is Copy, and a deferred call's Copy result, may be dropped without `discard`, as exceptions to OWN-7@3 (OD29). Bears on OWN-7, OWN-17, MEM-5 and OWN-15. The current compiler: both accepted: `free(h);` on a `Heap<Int>` (whose result is the `Int` moved out of the cell: `let v: Int = free(h);` after `alloc(42)` gives 42 on both consumers) and `defer f();` with `fn f() -> u8` compile and run, while OWN-7@3 (decided OD29) drops a non-Unit result only through `discard(e)`. OWN-17 states the current compiler's drop of a deferred call's result, and MEM-5 and OWN-15 describe `free(h);` as a statement; neither is named as an exception in OWN-7. Options: (a) both are exceptions, stated in OWN-7; (b) neither is, so each needs `discard`; (c) `free` is an exception and a deferred call's result is not. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised by the spec quality audit, 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-140 | see OPEN-140 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Neither; each needs `discard`. Earlier clarification of 2026-11-02: `free` no longer exists (OD59); a deferred call's Copy result is dropped with `discard`, while a `Result` or `Option` cannot be discarded (OD62) and is handled by a named function that returns `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority)). Revised 2026-11-02: `defer` takes a call whose result is `Unit` ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-audit)) **Canonical current deferred-result rule: the Unit-only audit rule below; the earlier discard-inside-defer fragment is superseded, and authority rule 9 refers to the Unit-only rule.** |

